  "knowledgePattern": "Turn circle tangency into a quadratic condition on the line gradient",
  "knowledgeTags": [
    "geometry"
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Translate the coordinates so B becomes the origin and the circle centre becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Translation leaves gradients unchanged. The radius is perpendicular to a tangent, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></msqrt><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. The translated circle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>A nonvertical line through the origin has equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Substitution gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Tangency means a repeated intersection root, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>160</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. This simplifies to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>The sum of its two gradient roots is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. They are 3 and 1/3, both valid tangent gradients. The vertical line x = 0 is not tangent, since its distance 5 from the centre exceeds the radius. Answer E.</p>",
  "hints": [
